-- Trade lifecycle - taking a commodity trade from booking to
-- settlement, with the rules about who may confirm a price and which
-- price may be settled on enforced by the runtime instead of by a
-- status column and a prayer.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. A trader strikes a deal - buy or sell a
-- quantity of some commodity (power, gas, oil) for delivery over a
-- future period - and *captures* it: the terms, plus the price they
-- believe they struck. That captured price is only the trader's word
-- for it. Before anyone settles money against the trade, a separate
-- desk - the middle office - independently *confirms* it against the
-- counterparty's own record, and the price they confirm becomes the
-- official one. The trade then *settles*. (Correction of an official
-- price and settlement itself arrive in later slices of this example;
-- capture and confirmation are modelled here.)
--
-- Two ideas do the real work, and this example is built around both.
--
-- First, a trade is never in a single "status". It is captured, then
-- also confirmed, then also settled - each step adds to what is true
-- about it, and the earlier facts never stop being true. There is no
-- `status = "confirmed"` field to overwrite: a trade is "confirmed"
-- precisely because a confirmation claim exists for it. The phase is
-- the accumulation of admitted claims, and the history reads itself.
--
-- Second, the same trade carries two prices at two standings. The
-- trader's captured price is an estimate - real, recorded, but not
-- something anyone may settle on. The middle office's confirmed price
-- is the official one, marked by a pointer to the figure currently in
-- force, and settlement will rely on that. Existence and
-- admissibility-for-settlement are different things: a captured price
-- exists without ever being settleable.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about this. It does not store "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the
-- rules. Two kinds of rule do the work:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one - the official price in force can never point at a
--     figure that does not exist.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked the moment you
--     act and never again. Whether the actor confirming a price holds
--     authority for that commodity is settled when they act; revoking
--     that authority tomorrow does not unwind a confirmation made
--     today. An invariant governs what is true; a gate governs what
--     you may do.

program trade_lifecycle

-- The vocabulary. `TradeCaptured` is the trade's terms; `CapturedPrice`
-- the trader's estimate, recorded but carrying no settlement standing.
-- `MayConfirm` is a desk's authority to confirm prices for a commodity
-- - it can be granted and, later, revoked. `TradeConfirmed` records the
-- confirmation event against the counterparty. `OfficialPrice` is an
-- official price figure; `CurrentOfficialPrice` is the retractable
-- pointer naming which official figure is in force for the trade right
-- now - the one moving part, which a later price correction will move.
predicate TradeCaptured(trade: Subject, commodity: Subject, direction: Subject, quantity: Decimal, delivery_period: Subject)
predicate CapturedPrice(trade: Subject, price: Decimal)
predicate MayConfirm(principal: Subject, commodity: Subject)
predicate TradeConfirmed(trade: Subject, counterparty: Subject, confirmation_id: Subject)
predicate OfficialPrice(trade: Subject, price: Decimal, official_price_id: Subject)
predicate CurrentOfficialPrice(trade: Subject, official_price_id: Subject)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - a trade was captured, an authority granted, a trade
-- confirmed.
intent TradeCapturedAdmitted(trade: Subject)
intent ConfirmAuthorityGranted(principal: Subject, commodity: Subject)
intent TradeConfirmedAdmitted(trade: Subject, confirmation_id: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. Any change that would leave the
-- world violating one of these is rejected outright. Read `implies` as
-- "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".

-- The official price in force can never point at nothing: every current
-- pointer must name an official price figure that actually exists. You
-- cannot end up settling on a price with no record behind it.
invariant current_official_price_has_a_figure:
    CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, official_price_id) implies OfficialPrice(trade, _, official_price_id)

-- There is exactly one official price in force at a time for a trade.
-- The pointer is a singleton: two different current official prices for
-- the same trade cannot coexist.
invariant at_most_one_current_official_price:
    CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, a) and CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, b) implies a = b


-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim; `emit` queues a message
-- to the outside world for after the commit.

-- A trader captures a new trade: its terms and the price they believe
-- they struck. The gate refuses a second capture under the same trade
-- id, so a trade's terms are fixed once booked - a change of terms is a
-- new trade, never a silent overwrite. The captured price is recorded
-- but carries no settlement standing on its own; only a confirmed price
-- will.
transformation capture_trade(trade, commodity, direction, quantity, delivery_period, price):
    require not TradeCaptured(trade, _, _, _, _)
    admit TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, direction, quantity, delivery_period)
    admit CapturedPrice(trade, price)
    emit TradeCapturedAdmitted(trade)

-- Grant a desk the authority to confirm prices for a commodity.
-- Confirmation is a middle-office responsibility, scoped per commodity
-- and kept separate from whoever captured the trade.
transformation grant_confirm_authority(principal, commodity):
    admit MayConfirm(principal, commodity)
    emit ConfirmAuthorityGranted(principal, commodity)

-- The middle office confirms the trade against the counterparty and
-- sets the official price. The first gate does two things at once: it
-- looks up the trade's commodity and checks that whoever is proposing
-- this holds confirmation authority for that commodity - so the
-- authority is tied to the trade's own commodity, not just any
-- commodity the actor happens to hold. The remaining gates insist the
-- trade has not already been confirmed and has no official price in
-- force yet. Confirming records the confirmation event, the official
-- price figure, and the pointer marking that figure as the one in force
-- for settlement.
transformation confirm_trade(trade, counterparty, confirmation_id, official_price_id, confirmed_price):
    require TradeCaptured(trade, commodity, _, _, _) and MayConfirm(actor, commodity)
    require not TradeConfirmed(trade, _, _)
    require not CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, _)
    admit TradeConfirmed(trade, counterparty, confirmation_id)
    admit OfficialPrice(trade, confirmed_price, official_price_id)
    admit CurrentOfficialPrice(trade, official_price_id)
    emit TradeConfirmedAdmitted(trade, confirmation_id)
